@@ -74,7 +74,7 @@ This document is the concise contract maintainers should update when public beha
 ## Stable Canonical Read Snapshots
 
 - Canonical reads and validation accept only one proven-stable generation. One attempt captures bounded root and kind entry name/type sets, reads records through verified no-follow descriptors, validates the graph and referenced artifacts, then rechecks all accepted evidence before returning anything.
-- Acceptance binds each record to its lossless metadata and raw-byte digest. Invalid, oversized, or unreadable entries that contributed validation output remain part of the generation authority, as do stable and invalid artifact observations with their verified ancestor chains. A changed entry, record, artifact, or accepted directory generation discards the complete attempt.
+- Acceptance binds each record to its lossless metadata and raw-byte digest. An unchanged attempt reads and hashes accepted canonical bytes once; closing checks compare the retained descriptor, pathname and directory-generation evidence without rereading those bytes. Accepting a cleanup-induced change time still requires the original bytes and digest to agree. Invalid, oversized, or unreadable entries that contributed validation output remain part of the generation authority, as do stable and invalid artifact observations with their shared verified ancestors. Unreadable evidence is probed again before acceptance. A changed entry, record, artifact, or accepted directory generation discards the complete attempt.
 - Public validation and canonical read helpers share one records-owned retry ledger. It permits at most three complete attempts within one non-resetting 60-second deadline, checked before a retry. Stable invalid repositories retain their ordinary validation result; repeated churn fails with concise path-free `REPOSITORY_CHANGED` rather than exposing partial records or stale validation output.
 - An internal immutable `VerifiedCorpus` carries the accepted records in creation-time/ID and ordinal-path order, shared ID and case-normalised path lookups, superseded/active IDs, active-head groups, deduplicated ordinal artifact paths, byte accounting, raw digest/metadata witnesses, and record-local summary/search projections. Canonical-owned payloads are frozen during the existing parser traversal; public results remain independent mutable values. Validation and generated-head allowances reuse the same indexes. Search body/preview strings are computed only when needed and reused for cache equality checks and writing.
 - Mutation planning can inspect stable but repairable active heads before validating the proposed union. A proposal does not represent published bytes: the committed corpus binds actual timestamps and raw publication witnesses, and its closing assertion captures fixed generation evidence. Cache recovery may reuse a replacement corpus only after reasserting that evidence; it does not repeat its canonical scan merely because disposable cache recovery is needed.
@@ -141,7 +141,7 @@ This document is the concise contract maintainers should update when public beha
 - Disposable cache recovery is limited to corrupt, not-a-database, schema, read-only, and cannot-open failures. Busy, locked, general I/O, and unknown failures are terminal for the operation; the operation gate separately reports busy or locked contention and recovers only corrupt or not-a-database state.
 - Public I/O wrapping recognises busy, locked, corrupt, not-a-database, read-only, cannot-open, and general I/O categories as environmental failures. Schema and unknown SQLite failures remain internal errors after any cache recovery is exhausted.
 - Freshness is determined from explicit cache metadata, a manifest of canonical records plus referenced artifacts, and an exact record-corpus fingerprint. The reserved `_staging` and `_artifacts` directory trees are excluded from the record-manifest portion, including their directory metadata and contents; referenced artifact files are included separately.
-- Artifact syntax remains a schema concern. Filesystem inspection captures the real `encephalon` root once per batch, binds each real non-link ancestor to its verified parent generation, and opens each final path read-only, nonblocking, without acquiring a controlling terminal, and with no-follow semantics wherever the runtime provides those flags. Initial and final descriptor/path metadata, ancestor revalidation, and post-revalidation descriptor/path metadata must agree before an immutable observation is accepted. After a real `encephalon` directory has been captured, every stable or invalid observation is re-inspected through the same primitive after initial batch collection: stable observations must retain identical complete metadata, invalid observations must remain invalid, and any kind transition is a repository change. A stable canonical-spelling mismatch is invalid, while a changed directory generation remains a repository change.
+- Artifact syntax remains a schema concern. One operation-local proof captures the real `encephalon` root and each unique real non-link ancestor, binding every child to its verified parent generation. Each unique final path is opened once read-only, nonblocking, without acquiring a controlling terminal, and with no-follow semantics wherever the runtime provides those flags. Initial and final descriptor/path metadata, parent revalidation, and post-revalidation descriptor/path metadata must agree. Closing checks revalidate the unique directory witnesses around retained leaf metadata and invalid-boundary evidence; they do not reopen files or reconstruct ancestor chains. Stable observations must retain identical complete metadata, invalid observations must retain their exact observed metadata or absence, and any kind transition is a repository change. A stable canonical-spelling mismatch is invalid, while a changed directory generation remains a repository change. The accepted canonical snapshot owns this proof and reuses it at query and publication boundaries; no proof survives into a different attempt.
 - Record validation performs artifact inspection once and hands both the verified observations and stable canonical scan evidence directly to cache-manifest construction; cache rebuild does not restat detached artifact paths or repeat the canonical scan. A statically missing, linked, or non-regular artifact is an invalid record artifact; replacement or mutation during inspection is a repository change for every canonical-validation consumer, including record addition and initialisation before publication, and remains subject to the bounded cache-rebuild retry policy. Genuine operational filesystem failures retain their I/O classification. Public messages, details, validation issues, and CLI output do not expose absolute artifact paths; retained internal diagnostic causes are outside that guarantee.
 - Guarded filesystem identity decisions share one internal authority that preserves device and inode values as `bigint` and compares stable metadata at nanosecond precision. Complete stable reads compare the full retained identity and metadata; parent-directory instance checks compare only device and inode after independently proving directory type, so ordinary child changes do not invalidate the parent; and post-rename publication checks omit only `ctimeNs`, whose change is expected after link or rename operations.
 - Manifest entry metadata comes from one stable filesystem inspection within captured real-parent generations. Root and kind witnesses surround enumerated record-entry inspection. Enumerated disappearance, link substitution, or parent-generation change is retried as a repository change, while genuine operational filesystem failures retain their I/O classification.
